if it affects anything more than the squares adjacent to you then
it is too powerful, _IMO_ .

the influence of conflict should be like a taming spell but not
permanent.  i still like the idea where on so-called "even" monster
moves only orthogonal adjacent monsters (i.e. those on the hjkl
squares) would be conflicted and would appear to fight on your
behalf if there were other monsters about (this includes attacking
your pet).  then on so-called "odd" monster moves only diagonally
adjacent monsters would be affected (i.e. those on the yubn squares).
if there is only one monster in the vicinity then the chances should
be good that it will still attack you (but perhaps it gets distracted
looking for other monsters during its turn).  your pet should also
be possibly disturbed by the conflict spell so you shouldn't just
keep it on.

this has the effect of allowing monsters to still have a shot at you
(which is really the point of the game) and still making the monsters
fight amongst themselves in an unpredicatable manner.  the field of
conflict would sort of pulsate like + x + x + x + in time.  you'd get
some benifit as the Lich takes out the orc-captain but you'd still
have to deal with the Lich, so conflict wouldn't be a cakewalk ...

just my opinion.

